FEATURES OF THE AMENDMENTS

1. A common criterion for determining the base of the tax to be paid on imported commodities, whether it be a customs duty or an advance sales tax has been adopted and this is the home consumption value.

2. The authority of the President under the flexibility clause has been expanded to include, subject to the limitation prescribed therein, authority to revise the taxable base levels of the percentage taxes for automobiles (Sec. 195) and certain semi-essential articles in Section 197.

3. The President is provided with a standby authority to subject the second sale of any article subject to percentage tax to a value-added tax, upon recommendation of the Secretary of Finance.

4. The tax on non-essential or luxury items was reduced from 70% to 50%, while the tax on semi-essentials or semi-luxuries, from 40% to 25%. The tax on ordinary items was increased from 7% to 10%.

5. A system of socialized pricing was introduced for certain commodities, whereby the tax is graduated on the basis of the price. Thus, Section 197 on integrated and non-integrated refrigeration products and Section 198 on locally manufactured electronic products under the Consolidated Tax Code of 1977 prior to this amendment, were both deleted and the articles enumerated in said sections were incorporated under the section on semi-essential items (Sec. 196) and the section on socialized pricing (Sec. 197). The selected semi-essential items which were removed from the purview of Section 196 and incorporated in a new section (Sec. 197) with progressive tax rates based on the concept of socialized pricing, are:

(1) Fountain pens and ball pens;

(2) Chairs, sofas, beds, desks and similar furnitures;

(3) Watches and clocks;

(4) Electric fans and exhaust fans;

(5) Electric gas and oil stoves and ranges;

(6) Radio-phono, tape recorders, video tape recorders, car stereos and similar articles for reproducing music and sound;

(7) Television sets;

(8) Refrigerators and freezers.

When the above-mentioned articles are imported, the tax is twenty-five (25%) per centum of landed cost plus mark-up.

6. The phrase "except those locally manufactured" immediately following certain articles enumerated under Section 194 (non-essential) and Section 196 (semi-essential) both under the Consolidated Tax Code of 1977 prior to this amendment, which in effect subject the particular locally manufactured articles to the ordinary rates was likewise deleted.

7. A percentage tax equivalent to one (1%) per centum of the gross selling price or gross value in money of agricultural products, whether food or non-food, other than those subject to tax under Section 201 and 203 has been imposed. If the article is imported the tax is ten (10%) per centum of landed cost plus mark-up.

8. The cost of raw material deduction method of computing the percentage tax due on the finished product has been replaced by the tax credit method. This means that any percentage, specific or mining tax paid on any material, part or accessory forming part of the finished article is allowed to be credited against the sales tax due on said article. Any excess tax credit may be carried over to the succeeding taxable quarter. When the raw material, part or accessory used in manufacturing is tax-exempt, the tax otherwise due thereon, shall be deemed paid for purposes of the tax credit.

9. The enumeration of essential articles subject to the 5% tax has been expanded to include locally manufactured medicine, laundry soap and detergents, writing pads, notebooks and ordinary lead pencils, and cement. Animal feeds however, was limited to feeds for poultry, swine and cattle excluding horse, cat and dog feeds.

10. A catch-all provision was installed in all the sales tax sections except Section 198 and Section 199 to enable the Secretary of Finance to add to the enumeration in the various sections similar or analogous articles on the basis of their inherent essentialities.

The Decree took effect April 21, 1978 except the provisions thereof relating to the computation and payment of the quarterly percentage tax on sales which shall take effect beginning July 1, 1978.

